"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][13]={"school_physics_en:M13-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M13-L1 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Two people each travel five metres. One ends five metres east of the\nstart; the other returns after walking a loop. Why is their distance\ntravelled the same while their displacement differs?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>scalar quantity<\/strong> has a numerical value and a unit\nbut no spatial direction. Examples include mass, temperature, energy and\ndistance travelled. A <strong>vector quantity<\/strong> also has\ndirection: displacement, velocity, acceleration and force. An arrow\nrepresents a vector: its length conveys magnitude and its direction\nconveys orientation. For forces, the point of application can also\nmatter.<\/p>\n<p>In perpendicular coordinates, a vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{A}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be described as the ordered pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(A_x,A_y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComponents have <strong>signs<\/strong>: a vector pointing to the left\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_x&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe magnitude is nonnegative:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\vec{A}|=\\sqrt{A_x^2+A_y^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Add vectors component by component:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>A<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{A}+\\vec{B}=(A_x+B_x,A_y+B_y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe head-to-tail graphical method yields the same resultant.\n<strong>Distance travelled<\/strong> is not\n<strong>displacement<\/strong>: displacement includes direction from the\ninitial to final position.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_076_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Cartesian axes showing a displacement arrow (3, 4) m with 3 m and 4 m components and magnitude 5 m.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Cartesian axes showing a displacement\narrow (3, 4) m with 3 m and 4 m components and magnitude 5\nm.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Displacement magnitude is 5 m; the two path segments cover 7\nm.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>An object moves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neast and then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnorth. With east as positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand north as positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>s<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec{s}=(3,4)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts magnitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>s<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\vec{s}|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}\\,\\mathrm m=5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe distance along the two straight sections is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3+4=7\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe displacement magnitude is therefore not the distance travelled.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper investigation:<\/strong> Draw axes with a ruler and plot\narrows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(3,4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin centimetres. Add them graphically and numerically, keeping axes and\nunits visible. No special equipment is required.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a temperature of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5\\,^\\circ\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na vector? Explain.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the magnitude and quadrant of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec a=(-6,8)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec a=(3,4)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec b=(-1,2)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How can displacement equal zero while distance travelled is\npositive?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. The minus sign indicates a point on a temperature scale, not a\nspatial direction.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\vec a|=10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nup and to the left (quadrant II).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>a<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mover><mi>b<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec a+\\vec b=(2,6)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A closed path ends at its starting position even though the\nindividual segments have positive lengths.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Components change when coordinate axes change, while magnitude does\nnot. Describing force effects may additionally require the point of\napplication.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n2.5.2–2.5.4: superposition, mathematics, prediction, uncertainty and\nmodels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1.1–3.1.2: motion, graphs, data analysis and uncertainty · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics/pages/2-2-vectors-scalars-and-coordinate-systems\">OpenStax\n– College Physics, 2.2 Vectors, Scalars and Coordinate Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 2.2: scalar/vector distinctions and reference directions · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M13-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M13-L2 – Functions, slopes and areas in graphs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does the slope of a position–time graph represent velocity,\nwhereas the area under a velocity–time graph represents\ndisplacement?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In physics a graph models a relationship between measured quantities.\n<strong>Axis labels and units<\/strong> determine what a slope or area\nmeans. For constant velocity,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x_0+vt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe position–time slope is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\frac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Its unit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA steeper positive slope means faster movement in the positive\ndirection; a negative slope indicates motion in the negative direction.\nOn a velocity–time graph, the <strong>signed area<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=\\int v(t)\\,dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives displacement (for constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsimply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\,\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nTotal distance travelled is the area under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|v|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead; forward and backward portions must not cancel. Area under an\nacceleration–time graph gives velocity change. For a curved line, the\ntangent slope represents an instantaneous value, to be developed further\nlater.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_077_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two motion graphs: a position line starting at 2 m with 3 m/s slope, and a velocity–time area of 12 m.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two motion graphs: a position line\nstarting at 2 m with 3 m/s slope, and a velocity–time area of 12\nm.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Position–time slope gives velocity, while velocity–time area\ngives displacement.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A cart starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand moves at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2+3\\cdot4=14\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe intercept of its\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe rectangular area under the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngraph from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthis is <strong>displacement<\/strong>, not the final position.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Table-to-graph exercise:<\/strong> On squared paper, plot\npositions\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2,5,8,11,14\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor times\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3,4\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOn a second graph plot constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLabel each axis with a quantity and unit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the unit of slope on a position–time graph?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=6\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1\\,\\mathrm m+2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}\\cdot t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A body travels for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFind displacement and distance.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes a secant from a tangent on a curved position–time\ngraph?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Metres per second\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=13\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Displacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ndistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A secant yields an average slope over an interval; a tangent yields\nan instantaneous slope.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>An area has physical meaning only when the axis quantities support\nthat interpretation. Do not treat scattered data points as a perfect\nline without justification.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n2.5.2–2.5.4: superposition, mathematics, prediction, uncertainty and\nmodels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1.1–3.1.2: motion, graphs, data analysis and uncertainty · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics/pages/2-8-graphical-analysis-of-one-dimensional-motion\">OpenStax\n– College Physics, 2.8 Graphical Analysis of One-Dimensional Motion<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Section 2.8: slope of position–time graphs and velocity interpretation\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M13-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M13-L3 – Trigonometric relationships in mechanics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A rope pulls a sled diagonally upward. Which portion pulls it\nhorizontally and which portion reduces its load on the ground?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be represented by <strong>projections<\/strong> onto two\nperpendicular axes. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis measured <strong>from the positive horizontal direction<\/strong>,\nthen<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=F\\cos\\alpha,\\qquad F_y=F\\sin\\alpha.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mover><mi>F<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=|\\vec F|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the magnitude. Signs depend on the quadrant and axis convention. For\nan incline at angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngravity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_G=mg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas components\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>∥<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,\\parallel}=mg\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndown the slope and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,\\perp}=mg\\cos\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto the slope. The normal force equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg\\cos\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n<strong>in magnitude only in a suitable situation<\/strong>, with no\nother perpendicular forces, no acceleration perpendicular to the surface\nand sustained contact. Resolving a force does not change it; components\nare an alternative description of the same vector.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_078_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"An angled 50 N force arrow resolved into approximately 43.3 N horizontal and 25 N vertical components.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">An angled 50 N force arrow resolved into\napproximately 43.3 N horizontal and 25 N vertical\ncomponents.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>For an angle to the horizontal F_x = F cos α and F_y = F sin\nα.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A rope pulls with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=50\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove the horizontal.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>43.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=50\\cos30^\\circ\\approx43.3\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_y=50\\sin30^\\circ=25\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe check\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msup><mn>43.3<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mn>25<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{43.3^2+25^2}\\approx50\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreproduces the original magnitude. The horizontal pulling component is\nless than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe diagram study:<\/strong> Draw a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvector and its right-angled component triangle. Measure the projected\narrow lengths with a ruler and compare the ratios with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNo real pulling apparatus is needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which component is adjacent to the angle measured from the\nhorizontal?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x,F_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=20\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirected\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nabove the positive horizontal.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=5\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincline, find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">mg\\sin30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=9.81\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=mg\\cos\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot always valid for an object in contact with an incline?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The horizontal component\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=F\\cos\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>17.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x\\approx17.3\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_y=10\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mo>∥<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>24.525<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>24.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{G,\\parallel}=24.525\\,\\mathrm N\\approx24.5\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndown the slope.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Additional forces, loss of contact or perpendicular acceleration can\nchange the normal force.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The trigonometric formula relies on defining the angle correctly.\nSigns and directions follow the selected axes: “sine is always the\nvertical component” is false.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n2.5.2–2.5.4: superposition, mathematics, prediction, uncertainty and\nmodels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1.1–3.1.2: motion, graphs, data analysis and uncertainty · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/college-physics/pages/2-2-vectors-scalars-and-coordinate-systems\">OpenStax\n– College Physics, 2.2 Vectors, Scalars and Coordinate Systems<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nSection 2.2: scalar/vector distinctions and reference directions · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M13-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M13-L4 – Measurement uncertainty, best-fit lines and model testing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Three stopwatches return slightly different times for one procedure.\nWhich number should be reported, and how confident can we be?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Measurement uncertainty<\/strong> characterises the spread of\nvalues plausibly attributable to a measurand; it is not a personal\nmistake and not automatically the same as one deviation. <strong>Random\ninfluences<\/strong> cause variation between repeats, while\n<strong>systematic influences<\/strong> can shift all readings together,\nfor example a misaligned zero. The mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>t<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∑<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar t=(1/n)\\sum t_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsummarises repeated values but does not remove systematic bias.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>best-fit line<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=ax+b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents a trend in measured data. Its slope has physical units (such\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a position–time graph). A sketched line should not be forced through\nevery data point. <strong>Residuals<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i=y_i-(ax_i+b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare the differences between observations and predicted values.\nStructured residual patterns suggest the model may be inadequate. An\nobserved spread does <strong>not automatically imply a 95% confidence\ninterval<\/strong>; statistical assumptions and procedures are needed for\nsuch a claim.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_079_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three measured times from 1.98 to 2.02 s, plus a separate position–time scatterplot and approximate trend line.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three measured times from 1.98 to 2.02 s,\nplus a separate position–time scatterplot and approximate trend\nline.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Repeated readings exhibit variation, and a model line is only an\napproximation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Repeated measurements give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.98<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_1=1.98\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_2=2.02\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_3=2.00\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>t<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar t=2.00\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the range is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.04<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.04\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor these three readings the <strong>sample standard deviation<\/strong>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.98<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2.02<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=\\sqrt{[(1.98-2)^2+(2.02-2)^2+0]/(3-1)}=0.02\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis describes variation; a full uncertainty statement would also\nconsider instrument properties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Data exercise:<\/strong> Draw several position–time\nmeasurements for a nearly constant-speed toy on squared paper. Sketch a\nplausible straight line through the cloud. Compute its slope from two\nwidely separated points <strong>on the line<\/strong>, rather than\nselecting arbitrary individual observations. Mark any unusual\nresiduals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does averaging not fix a stopwatch with a shifted zero?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the mean of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.9\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does a slope of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmean for a position–time best-fit line?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a curved residual pattern a warning sign?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A shared systematic offset persists in the average.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>t<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar t=2.0\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The line models constant velocity of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It suggests that a linear model may not capture the data-generating\nrelationship.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The three-reading example is an introduction, not a complete\nuncertainty budget compliant with metrology standards. Precise coverage\nstatements require raw data, calibration evidence and assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1.1–3.1.2: motion, graphs, data analysis and uncertainty · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/nist-technical-note-1297/nist-tn-1297-appendix-d1-terminology\">NIST\n– Technical Note 1297, Appendix D1: Terminology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section D1:\nuncertainty, random and systematic measurement errors · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n2.5.2–2.5.4: superposition, mathematics, prediction, uncertainty and\nmodels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M13-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M13-L5 – Digital measurement, data analysis and simulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A motion sensor can record hundreds of readings every second. Does\nthat automatically produce a more accurate physical model?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Digital measurement systems sample quantities at <strong>discrete\ntime steps<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <strong>sampling frequency<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=1/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not the same as measurement accuracy. Consecutive positions give\ninterval-average velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_i=(x_{i+1}-x_i)/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA finite interval yields an <strong>average<\/strong>, not an exact\ninstantaneous velocity. Shorter intervals improve time resolution but\nmay amplify fluctuations when differences are calculated from noisy\npositions.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>simulation<\/strong> computes predictions from specified\nequations and initial conditions. It is neither a real observation nor\nproof that the governing equations are correct. Compare three columns:\nmeasured values, model predictions and residuals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_i=x_{\\mathrm{meas},i}-x_{\\mathrm{model},i}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nChecks include units, timestamps, sensor calibration, noise, assumptions\nand independent plausibility tests. More points may simply repeat the\nsame systematic error many times.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_080_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Quadratic model curve with four synthetic measured positions and a measured-versus-model table.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Quadratic model curve with four synthetic\nmeasured positions and a measured-versus-model table.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Digital readings are compared against predictions without\nconflating a simulation and a measurement.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For the <strong>idealised<\/strong> relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=t^2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis in seconds, positions at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0,1,4,9\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe interval-average velocities for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthey belong to intervals, not the individual start times. The\ncorresponding model has constant acceleration\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nReal sensor readings would generally deviate slightly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Spreadsheet exercise:<\/strong> In a local spreadsheet enter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsynthetic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3.8<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>9.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{meas}}=0.1;1.1;3.8;9.2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{model}}=t^2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate differences and plot the measured points together with the\nseparate model curve. These are <strong>synthetic values<\/strong>, not\nrecorded sensor data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_s=20\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate interval-average velocity from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(1)=1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(2)=4\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the residual for measured\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.8\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nversus predicted\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why do 10,000 simulated points not prove a model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=1/20\\,\\mathrm s=0.05\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=(4-1)/(2-1)=3\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=3.8-4.0=-0.2\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The values are generated by the chosen equations; independent\nmeasurements and model checks remain necessary.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Smaller sampling steps do not guarantee accuracy. Finite differences\namplify measurement noise; every simulation is bounded by its\nassumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1.1–3.1.2: motion, graphs, data analysis and uncertainty · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://lehrplannavigator.nrw.de/system/files/media/document/file/gost_ph_klp_beitraege_medienkompetenzrahmen_nrw_synopse_2022_06_09.pdf\">QUA-LiS\nNRW – Media competence and upper-secondary Physics curriculum (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Introductory phase: digital acquisition, mathematical modelling, data\ndisplays and evaluation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n2.5.2–2.5.4: superposition, mathematics, prediction, uncertainty and\nmodels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M13-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M13-L6 – Scientific argumentation and model limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Vectors, scalars and components<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Four points lie almost on a straight line. Is that enough to claim\nthat the object moves at a perfectly constant speed everywhere and\nforever?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A defensible physical argument distinguishes <strong>claim, evidence,\nreasoning and limitation<\/strong>. A claim might be: “Over the observed\ninterval the motion is approximately uniform.” Evidence consists of data\nwith units and uncertainties. Reasoning connects them to the model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=x_0+vt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand examines residuals. Limitations specify the time range, apparatus\nand alternative explanations.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>model<\/strong> is a purposeful approximation: the\nconstant-<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodel may describe a short segment even though starting, stopping or\nchanging forces require another model. Good agreement is not a logical\nproof for all circumstances. When evaluating a graph, examine axis\nscaling, units, error bars, sample size, selective reporting and the\ndifference between observation and interpretation.\n<strong>Correlation<\/strong> alone does not establish causation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_081_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four boxes labelled claim, evidence, reasoning and limitation, with a linear model and its residuals.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four boxes labelled claim, evidence,\nreasoning and limitation, with a linear model and its\nresiduals.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A qualified physical conclusion must consider both evidence and\nmodel assumptions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0,1,2,3\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsuppose <strong>synthetic<\/strong> positions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3.4<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>5.6<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>7.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1.5;3.4;5.6;7.4\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{model}}=1.5\\,\\mathrm m+(2\\,\\mathrm{m/s})t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npredicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>5.5<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mn>7.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5;3.5;5.5;7.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nResiduals (observed minus predicted) are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>;<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0;-0.1;+0.1;-0.1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA suitable conclusion is “compatible with approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover the measured range”, not “exactly constant without error\nforever”.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Argumentation workshop:<\/strong> One group drafts the\nstrongest conclusion supported by these four synthetic points; the other\nidentifies assumptions and credible alternative models. Both groups then\npropose extra data and an independent check beyond the current time\nwindow.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give a physical claim and one measurement-based piece of evidence\nfor it.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the residual for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{meas}}=5.6\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_{\\mathrm{model}}=5.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional experiment could test constant velocity more\nstrongly?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does a similar curve shape not establish causation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example: “Velocity is approximately constant”; evidence:\npositions increase by similar amounts in equal time intervals.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=+0.1\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Collect repeated readings over a longer interval, ideally with a\ndifferent technique and documented uncertainty.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Confounding variables, alternative explanations or chance may create\na relationship without the proposed causal mechanism.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A few readings justify only a limited claim. Without uncertainty\nassessment, repetitions and independent checks, strong claims about\naccuracy or causation are not supported.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M14&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M14-L1 – Motion with constant\nacceleration<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for the German Abitur (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n2.5.2–2.5.4: superposition, mathematics, prediction, uncertainty and\nmodels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/fileadmin/bbb/unterricht/rahmenlehrplaene/gymnasiale_oberstufe/curricula/2022/Teil_C_RLP_GOST_2022_Physik.pdf\">Berlin-Brandenburg\n– Upper-secondary Physics curriculum, Part C (2022)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections\n3.1.1–3.1.2: motion, graphs, data analysis and uncertainty · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/nist-technical-note-1297/nist-tn-1297-appendix-d1-terminology\">NIST\n– Technical Note 1297, Appendix D1: Terminology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Section D1:\nuncertainty, random and systematic measurement errors · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
